    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `unmangle`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `unmangle` function is designed to process a given name to potentially reveal its original, un-mangled form if it was originally mangled due to Python's name mangling rules. This is particularly useful in the context of Sphinx's autodoc extension, where accurate names are crucial for generating documentation. The function takes two parameters: `subject`, which is the object or class that the name is associated with, and `name`, which is the string name that may be mangled. The function returns either the unmangled name if applicable, or the original name if it was not mangled or could not be unmangled. It relies on the `isclass` helper function to determine if the subject is a class and uses the `__name__` and `__mro__` attributes to check for mangled names. If an `AttributeError` is encountered during these checks, the function simply passes and returns the original name. The function does not have any side effects.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"